isl: never advertise the Xe2 CCS DRM modifiers

Userspace half of kernel-patches/7.1/chromeos/0002-lnl_bmg_ccs_deadvertise.patch,
which removes the same two modifiers from the i915 display planes.

Every ARC window rendered as vertical stripe noise with the coarse layout still
recognisable. The consumer on this system is a ChromeOS R149 image whose
minigbm predates Lunar Lake and has no entry for I915_FORMAT_MOD_4_TILED_LNL_CCS:
when the guest negotiates that layout for a buffer it exports, Chrome imports it
as plain 4-tiled and reads the compressed bytes as if they were pixels.

This replaces the INTEL_DEBUG=noccs that brunch-patches/87-arcvm_seccomp.sh was
exporting into the crosvm process tree. noccs was empirically effective but far
too broad: DEBUG_NO_CCS has exactly three effects (grep DEBUG_NO_CCS in
src/intel) --

  1. isl.c:3902        -- disables aux surfaces everywhere,
  2. anv_device.c:1554 -- disables PAT-memtype compression,
  3. isl_drm.c         -- removes the CCS modifiers from the advertised list.

Only (3) is load-bearing here. (2) is provably unreachable for a bo that will be
shared: anv_image_is_pat_compressible() returns false for any image with
external_handle_types or DRM-modifier tiling, so a compressed memory type is
never even offered for one -- confirmed by aggregating every host-side
vkAllocateMemory under a venus guest, where compressed and exported never
co-occur. (1) is pure loss: it costs bandwidth on everything the VM draws,
including surfaces that never leave the process.

Measured on 8086:64a0 by enumerating VkDrmFormatModifierPropertiesListEXT:

  stock, no noccs   LINEAR, X_TILED, 4_TILED, 4_TILED_LNL_CCS   (1 of 4 CCS)
  stock, noccs      LINEAR, X_TILED, 4_TILED                    (0 of 3)
  patched, no noccs LINEAR, X_TILED, 4_TILED                    (0 of 3)

i.e. the patch reproduces exactly the modifier list noccs produced, while
leaving render target compression, fast clears and sampler decompression alone.

BMG is de-advertised for the same reason and for symmetry with the kernel patch;
this package only ever installs on Lunar Lake, so it is not reachable in
practice.

--- a/src/intel/isl/isl_drm.c
+++ b/src/intel/isl/isl_drm.c
@@ -292,21 +292,27 @@ isl_drm_modifier_get_score(const struct intel_device_info *devinfo,

       return 5;
    case I915_FORMAT_MOD_4_TILED_LNL_CCS:
-      if (devinfo->ver < 20 || devinfo->has_local_mem)
-         return 0;
-
-      if (INTEL_DEBUG(DEBUG_NO_CCS))
-         return 0;
-
-      return 4;
+      /* brunch: never advertise the Xe2 CCS modifiers.
+       *
+       * This is the userspace half of
+       * kernel-patches/7.1/chromeos/0002-lnl_bmg_ccs_deadvertise.patch, which
+       * removes the same two modifiers from the display planes. The consumer
+       * on this system is a ChromeOS R149 image whose minigbm predates Lunar
+       * Lake and has no entry for them, so when the guest negotiates a CCS
+       * layout for a buffer it exports, Chrome imports it as plain 4-tiled and
+       * reads the compressed bytes as if they were pixels -- which is why
+       * every ARC window rendered as vertical stripe noise with the coarse
+       * layout still intact.
+       *
+       * Only the *shared* layout is given up. Internal CCS -- render target
+       * compression, fast clears, sampler decompression -- is untouched, which
+       * is what makes this preferable to INTEL_DEBUG=noccs: that also disables
+       * aux surfaces everywhere (isl.c:3902) and costs bandwidth on everything
+       * the VM draws, not just on buffers that cross a process boundary.
+       */
+      return 0;
    case I915_FORMAT_MOD_4_TILED_BMG_CCS:
-      if (devinfo->ver < 20 || !devinfo->has_local_mem)
-         return 0;
-
-      if (INTEL_DEBUG(DEBUG_NO_CCS))
-         return 0;
-
-      return 4;
+      return 0;
    }
 }

